Object Query
By Ken Fishkin.
These operations are performed using the refAny field of the cube to store a pointer
to a record containing a list of all objects which (at least partially) are within this cube.
This is NIL for non-terminal cubes.
PrintBox: PROC [b: Box, s: IO.STREAM, hdr: ROPE ¬ NIL]  ~ {
IF hdr # NIL THEN IO.PutRope[s,hdr];
G3dIO.PrintTriple[s,b.min,"min:"];
G3dIO.PrintTriple[s,b.max,"max:"];
};
first, go through and find the bounding box of all the objects: needed to create the Cube.
cube size is the max side length of the bbox:
sanity checks....
this can happen after subdividing. Set up an empty track, and fall into regular case.
does this object belong within me?
I am a terminal: add to me directly.
add to the kids. Since this guy can cross cube boundaries, might be added to more
than one child: go through all of them.
Subdivide the given cube (which must have been created with FromObjects/AddObject) until every sub-cube has either
1) <= (maxPerCube) objects in it
2) a depth = (maxDepth)

can't weasel out of it anymore: have to split myself up, and hand off the objects to my kids. Since my objects may cross more than one of my kids, will do this by adding each object to each kid: the kid will check and see if it lies outside his boundaries.
PrintBox[BoxOfCube[cube],TerminalIO.TOS[], IO.PutFR["%g: %g objs: Subdividing:", IO.int[cube.level], IO.int[nObjs]]];
and now fall down to non-terminal case (I'm not a terminal anymore):
need to make sure the kids aren't overloaded. Note that I don't have to change my bounding box, as the objects are unchanged and hence have the same bounding boxes.
G3dIO.PrintTriple[TerminalIO.TOS[], pt, "QueryObjects:pt:"];
PrintBox[BoxOfCube[cube], TerminalIO.TOS[], "checking against cube:"];
Figure out which octant could contain this point:
